    endText += `<p>The moment the Central Election Commission website crashed, Tsai Ing-wen happened to be using a ballpoint pen with peeling paint to miswrite a character on a sticky note.</p>`;

    endText += `<p>She had originally wanted to write out the word “Win” from memory just to see if she remembered the stroke order—a mindless action while waiting for the count, like someone doodling geometric shapes in the margins of meeting notes. But she placed the top dot in the wrong order, making it look like a wobbling stick figure. She paused for a second, circled the ink blob with the tail of the pen, and wrote a neat “Win” beside it, as if correcting a twenty-year-old homework mistake.</p>`;

    endText += `<p>On television, the news anchors’ voices jostled against each other on the same channel, every mention of “latest counting figures” sounding like someone adding a spoonful of hot chili oil to a boiling hotpot. Someone turned the volume up slightly; she didn't stop them or complain. To her, this noise was no different from a summer afternoon thunderstorm beating against arcade overhangs—listen long enough, and it possessed a strange, comforting calm.</p>`;

    endText += `<p>Her aides had squeezed into the lounge half an hour before polls closed, some clutching laptops, others holding cups of tea that had long gone cold, and one holding half a bag of unfinished dried squid, the wrapper rustling softly. Seeing the squid, her eyes crinkled slightly. “Give me a strip of that,” she said. The aide froze for a second before frantically handing the whole bag over. She pulled out a strip, chewing slowly, her eyes never leaving the television screen.</p>`;

    endText += `<p>The progress bar on screen shed skin like a snake; with every new set of figures stripped away, the underlying shade of blue faded another notch. The column next to Ma Ying-jeou's photo, which initially barely held above the opposite shoulder, collapsed downward—so fast that the broadcast director seemed unsure where to cut. As the landslide became unmistakable, the broadcast cut to a campaign truck parked by the roadside. A worker was using a utility knife to slice open the giant poster of Ma Ying-jeou's smiling face—one slash dividing his gold-rimmed glasses straight across the bridge of his nose.</p>`;

    endText += `<p>Tsai paused her chewing for half a beat, set the remaining half-strip beside her sticky note, and remarked quietly: “That blade is quite sharp.”</p>`;

    endText += `<p>When the secretary-general pushed the door open, a stray piece of confetti dropped by supporters outside was stuck to the sole of his leather shoe, scraping lightly against the wooden floor like fingernails on paper. He reported in a low voice: “Ma's campaign press room has already cleared away the snacks. Word is they prepared eighty wheel cakes, now all piled up in cardboard boxes in the hallway.”</p>`;

    endText += `<p>Tsai echoed: “Eighty.” It was a statement, not a question, like reciting a number on an invoice header. Then she flipped the sticky note over to its blank side, tapping the tip of the pen twice. “In 2012, they served hot grass jelly, didn't they?” She smiled briefly—like a door pushed open three inches by the wind, then softly pulled shut again.</p>`;

    endText += `<p>Her young aides exchanged glances—a look that translated to: <em>The Chairperson is in a remarkably good mood today.</em> It wasn't the hyper-active thrill of victory, but the quiet satisfaction of a retired teacher watching students answer every exam question correctly. She even joked when a political commentator on TV used the word “corroded” to describe Ma’s falling momentum, reaching out to make a faint grabbing motion in mid-air: “Looks like the Golden Boy’s expiration date wasn't printed on the can.”</p>`;

    endText += `<p>A suppressed chuckle leaked out from the room, sounding like soda fizzing from a loosened bottle cap.</p>`;

    endText += `<p>The next few hours felt less like watching election results and more like reviewing an audit forced upon her life. The 2012 rain, that cold wet night, hair soaked against her cheeks, and the raspy tear in her voice when she said “a noble defeat”—all of it was being crossed out line by line by tonight’s figures, so cleanly that not even eraser shavings needed to be blown away.</p>`;

    endText += `<p>When <strong style='color:#D32F2F; font-size:1.2em;'>${FINAL_VOTE_PERCENT.toFixed(1)}%</strong> finally appeared on the official CEC website, the room went vacuum-silent for half a second before a roar erupted from deep within everyone's throats. Her running mate <strong style='color:#0046AD;'>${currentMateName}</strong> called from the war room on another floor, voice flattened by radio waves, but the joy intact: “Chairperson, the south is locked in, the central region flipped by a margin bigger than we predicted, and the north is—to put it bluntly, a complete landslide.”</p>`;

    endText += `<p>Tsai cradled the phone on her shoulder while pushing the squid strip toward the corner of the table so it wouldn't get soaked by an overturned tea cup. She listened and replied: “Understood. Is there water over there? Drink some; you've spoken enough today.” Hanging up, she turned off the screen. Her lock screen was still the plump orange cat sticker sent by her sister hours ago. She unlocked it and replied with a single character: “Approved.”—as if approving a proposal known only to herself.</p>`;

    endText += `<p>Meanwhile, the television broadcast a side angle of Ma Ying-jeou leaving Kuomintang headquarters. His posture remained upright, his stride maintaining a presidential rhythm, but she noticed his right hand remained tucked deep inside his jacket pocket, never coming out. She suddenly remembered the 2012 television debate, when he had tapped the podium twice with that exact same hand, telling the camera: “My administration's achievements, everyone can see.” That gesture had been described by the media as “the beat of confidence.” Now, that same hand was hidden in his pocket, like hiding a mechanical watch unbuckled from the wrist.</p>`;

    endText += `<p>She didn't voice this observation. She simply stood up and walked to the window, pushing open a crack in the fogged glass. Outside on Beiping East Road, the crowd had begun singing spontaneously—lively, almost rough rhythm. Watching the campaign banners swell and collapse in the wind, she saw a staff worker pull a “Taiwan Must Win” flag out of the ground too quickly, bringing up a clump of wet soil that revealed the pink, squirming body of an earthworm. The worker didn't notice, but she whispered softly from the second-floor window: “There's an earthworm under your foot.” The glass was too thick; he couldn't hear her.</p>`;

    endText += `<p>Someone who has lost thoroughly always notices these small things unrelated to victory or defeat. Perhaps because during those nights of crushing defeat, the squirming of tiny creatures was all that remained to remind you: you are still alive, and you must be careful not to step on other living things that are trying to survive as well.</p>`;

    endText += `<p>Ma Ying-jeou’s concession statement was issued near ten o'clock in the evening. Under three hundred words long, formatted neatly in standard PMingLiU font, it looked like an unapproved administrative document. The final paragraph read: “I accept the election results and hereby retire from politics. Success and failure, I leave to history.”</p>`;

    endText += `<p>Tsai read the statement in the car on her way back to the official residence. The streetlights along Ren’ai Road cast tree shadows across the car window in steady frames, recalling 2012—the same road, the same shadows. Except that night, her hands had been freezing cold, clutching the fabric of her trousers tightly; tonight, her palms were warm, the glow of her phone screen reflecting at the bottom of her glasses like a calm lake. She read the statement twice, pausing longest on the phrase “leave to history,” then exited the app and set her phone on the seat. Passing a bus stop, she caught sight of a Ma campaign poster with a torn corner, a dead leaf stuck right over his gold-rimmed glasses.</p>`;

    endText += `<p>She suddenly spoke up: “Driver, please pull over at the convenience store ahead.”</p>`;

    endText += `<p>She didn't get out when the car stopped, merely rolling down the window to ask a bodyguard to buy a bottle of hot tea. The bodyguard returned holding the tea along with a small bag of soda crackers, saying the convenience store clerk had insisted on adding them: “She said it's her treat for the Chairperson.” Tsai carefully examined the handwritten note from the clerk, scribbled in ballpoint pen: “Thank you for your hard work.” She folded the note neatly and tucked it into her inner jacket pocket.</p>`;

    endText += `<p>As the car started up again, she opened the crackers. A small crumb fell onto the knee of her skirt. She picked it up with her fingertips and, instead of brushing it away, placed it gently on the rim of the car’s ashtray like an exhibit. When an aide asked her about it later, she thought for a moment and said: “Because it’s part of the land. Crumbs are too.”</p>`;

    endText += `<p>The final public event of the night was speaking to the crowd outside headquarters. Her prepared speech was two pages long, but a minute before stepping onto the stage, she folded the paper and slipped it into <strong style='color:#0046AD;'>${currentMateName}</strong>’s hand, keeping only a sticky note scribbled with a few keywords. Testing the microphone with two light taps, she addressed the sea of lights before her:</p>`;

    endText += `<p>“Tonight, I am not thinking of the joy of victory. I am thinking of that earthworm almost stepped on when a worker pulled up a flag.”</p>`;

    endText += `<p>The crowd quieted for a moment, thinking they had misheard. She continued: “I think, beneath such a massive victory, there must be many things in the soil that we have yet to see. They won't appear on election night, but on every future rainy night, they will turn up onto the asphalt alongside the earthworms.” She didn't explain the connection between the two sentences, merely slowing her pace: “Thank you for giving someone who once lost so thoroughly the chance to know what winning feels like. But more importantly, do not forget that beneath the soil of our victory, life remains. Those lives are everything.”</p>`;

    endText += `<p>When her speech concluded, she did not raise her hands in triumph. Instead, she gently held up a handwritten banner passed up from the crowd, bearing eight characters: <em>The Inner Demon Has Receded, Taiwan Walks Together.</em> Recognizing Chen Chu’s distinctive handwriting, the corners of her lips pulled into a faint, gentle curve.</p>`;

    endText += `<p>Returning to her official residence past midnight, she slipped off her leather shoes at the entryway. As her bare feet touched the cool floor tiles, she finally let out a long, quiet sigh. On the coffee table lay a half-read book, with a note tucked between its pages—a memo she had written to herself three days after her 2012 defeat, bearing just ten characters:</p>`;

    endText += `<p><em>“Next time it rains, remember to bring an umbrella.”</em></p>`;

    endText += `<p>She picked up the yellowed note, spun it between her fingers, and tucked it into another book—a newly bought science book on Taiwanese soil ecology, right at the chapter titled “Earthworms and Compost.” Finishing this, she switched off the last lamp in the living room. Outside the window, Taipei remained brightly lit, like a massive, silent election machine slowly scanning answer sheets for the next test, whose questions no one yet knew.</p>`;

